# Normalise line endings in the working tree, not just in the index.
#
# The Windows CI matrix leg failed three times at the formatting check,
# and the log finally said why: actions/checkout had produced CRLF files,
# and gofmt normalises CRLF to LF, so every file in ./cmd and ./internal
# compared as unformatted -- 60 files, all of them correct.
#
# The earlier two attempts were wrong and the logs are worth recording:
# the first was a `[ -n "$x" ]` test that pwsh cannot parse, and the
# second added `shell: bash` and still failed, for a reason that had
# nothing to do with the shell. Both looked like a shell problem because
# the failing step was the one that used a shell.
#
# The right fix is the one that makes the checkout correct on every
# platform rather than making the check tolerate a broken checkout. Go
# source is LF by convention, `go fmt` assumes it, and a Go repository
# where `git diff` shows every line as changed on Windows is a
# repository where nobody reviews the diff.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF even on Windows, or the shebang is preceded by
# a CR and the kernel refuses to run them.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The tape file is read line by line by vhs.
*.tape text eol=lf

# Binary fixtures. These are fetched verbatim from upstream and compared
# byte-for-byte by the corpus tests, so any normalisation would change
# what is being tested -- including the deliberately malformed fuzz
# crashers, whose exact bytes are the point.
*.pyc binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
*.png binary
*.gif binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# SBOM and report outputs. A JSON document with CRLF is valid but will not
# match a committed golden file, and a SARIF file is uploaded and diffed.
*.json text eol=lf
*.sarif text eol=lf

# testdata/ is already covered by `* text=auto`, which for a file git
# detects as binary leaves it alone. Stated explicitly because the corpus
# is the regression net and a normalisation bug there would be silent.
testdata/** -text
